Mixed models overall
For each population and outcome, this fits the model once with fit_mixed_model(), then makes its coefficient table with tabulate_model_terms() and its estimates with estimate_effects(). Read the nested brackets as a filing system: population, then outcome, then analysis. For example, mixed_model_tables[["itt"]][["act_score"]][["overall"]] selects the overall intention-to-treat ACT table after an authorized run. These are coefficient tables, not a single intervention-effect estimate per visit.
The model and what its coefficients mean are explained in Longitudinal data and mixed models.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Visit by arm, for each outcome
for (population in names(mixed_model_data)) {
  mixed_model_fits[[population]] &lt;- list()
  mixed_model_tables[[population]] &lt;- list()
  mixed_model_estimates[[population]] &lt;- list()

  for (outcome in repeated_outcomes) {
    fit &lt;- fit_mixed_model(mixed_model_data[[population]], outcome)

    mixed_model_fits[[population]][[outcome]] &lt;- list(overall = fit)
    mixed_model_tables[[population]][[outcome]] &lt;- list(
      overall = tabulate_model_terms(fit, paste0(outcome, " vs. time"))
    )
    mixed_model_estimates[[population]][[outcome]] &lt;- list(
      overall = estimate_effects(fit)
    )
  }
}

Mixed models subgroups
The extra loop asks whether the arm-by-visit pattern differs by one baseline subgroup at a time. Children and adults are compared only for outcomes used in both groups; the adult and caregiver quality-of-life measures are not interchangeable. A missing outcome or model predictor can change the rows used by a particular fit, so the same dataset name does not guarantee the same analysis sample for every outcome.
Reading the three-way visit × arm × subgroup terms is covered in Interactions, and the meaning of their p-values in What a p-value for interaction means.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Visit by arm by subgroup, for each outcome; child or adult only for
# outcomes measured in both groups
subgroup_analyses &lt;- c(
  lapply(repeated_outcomes, function(outcome) {
    list(outcome = outcome, subgroups = mixed_subgroup_variables)
  }),
  lapply(child_adult_mixed_outcomes, function(outcome) {
    list(outcome = outcome, subgroups = "child_adult")
  })
)

for (population in names(mixed_model_data)) {
  for (analysis in subgroup_analyses) {
    outcome &lt;- analysis$outcome

    for (subgroup in analysis$subgroups) {
      fit &lt;- fit_mixed_model(
        mixed_model_data[[population]], outcome,
        modifier = subgroup
      )

      mixed_model_fits[[population]][[outcome]][[subgroup]] &lt;- fit
      mixed_model_tables[[population]][[outcome]][[subgroup]] &lt;-
        tabulate_model_terms(
          fit, paste0(outcome, " vs. time * ", subgroup)
        )
      mixed_model_estimates[[population]][[outcome]][[subgroup]] &lt;-
        estimate_effects(fit, modifier = subgroup)
    }
  }
}

Count models
Each input row represents one participant, with a count and a follow-up duration. A for loop repeats the same formula for each outcome named in count_outcomes. glm.nb() fits a negative binomial model, which allows more variation in counts than a Poisson model. offset(log(followup_time_itt)) accounts for exposure time with its coefficient fixed at one. tbl_regression(exp = TRUE) converts log-scale coefficients to rate ratios, and tbl_stack() combines the outcome tables.
An invented rate ratio of 0.80 means a rate 20% lower in the intervention arm; it does not mean 20% fewer participants had an event. The confidence interval describes the precision of that estimated ratio. See the MASS function reference for fitting details (MASS authors, n.d.).
Why the offset turns counts into rates, and why the exponentiated coefficients are incidence rate ratios, is in Longitudinal data and mixed models.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Negative binomial regression of each count on arm, with log follow-up time
# as an offset; incidence rate ratios, one row per outcome
count_model_table &lt;- NULL

for (outcome in count_outcomes) {
  outcome_formula &lt;- paste0(
    outcome, " ~ ", "studyarm + offset(log(followup_time_itt))"
  ) %&gt;%
    as.formula()

  outcome_table &lt;- glm.nb(outcome_formula, data = rescue_medication_merged) %&gt;%
    tbl_regression(
      exp = TRUE,
      show_single_row = "studyarm",
      label = list(studyarm ~ outcome)
    ) %&gt;%
    modify_header(label = "**Outcome**")

  if (is.null(count_model_table)) {
    count_model_table &lt;- outcome_table
  } else {
    count_model_table &lt;- tbl_stack(list(count_model_table, outcome_table))
  }
}

Count interaction models
These loops add one subgroup at a time; they do not fit all subgroup variables together. studyarm * subgroup includes the arm term, subgroup term and their interaction. The named list stores each table first by outcome and then by subgroup, making the analysis that generated it identifiable. Read the reference category before interpreting the arm coefficient: it now applies within that category.
What each coefficient of an arm × subgroup model means is in Interactions, what the interaction p-value tests in What a p-value for interaction means, and how to get the IRR in each subgroup level in Effects behind an interaction.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Arm-by-subgroup interactions for each count outcome
count_interaction_tables &lt;- list()

for (outcome in count_outcomes) {
  count_interaction_tables[[outcome]] &lt;- list()

  for (subgroup in count_subgroup_variables) {
    outcome_formula &lt;- paste0(
      outcome, " ~ ", "studyarm*", subgroup,
      " + offset(log(followup_time_itt))"
    ) %&gt;%
      as.formula()

    count_interaction_tables[[outcome]][[subgroup]] &lt;-
      glm.nb(outcome_formula, data = rescue_medication_merged) %&gt;%
      tbl_regression(exp = TRUE)
  }
}

# Baseline asthma control, not fitted for hospitalizations
for (outcome in act_subgroup_count_outcomes) {
  for (subgroup in c("act_binary")) {
    outcome_formula &lt;- paste0(
      outcome, " ~ ", "studyarm*", subgroup,
      " + offset(log(followup_time_itt))"
    ) %&gt;%
      as.formula()

    count_interaction_tables[[outcome]][[subgroup]] &lt;-
      glm.nb(outcome_formula, data = rescue_medication_merged) %&gt;%
      tbl_regression(exp = TRUE) %&gt;%
      modify_caption(paste0("**", outcome, "**"))
  }
}

The trajectory figure, in parts
The figure is built from small parts, each returning an ordinary ggplot, and one function that arranges them. Someone who wants only the means, or a different arrangement, can use the parts directly. None of them fits a model: they draw the data and the estimates from estimate_effects().

Shared helpers
add_plot_offset() shifts each arm a little left or right of its visit, so the two arms’ points and bars sit side by side. One offset serves both the individual trajectories and the model means. The other helpers set the visits on the x-axis, format p-values, and pick out one subgroup level.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Shift each arm a little left or right of its visit, so the arms' points and
# bars sit side by side instead of on top of each other. One offset serves
# the individual trajectories and the model means (issue #12).
add_plot_offset &lt;- function(data, offset = 0.18, exposure = "studyarm",
                            time = "time_linear") {
  exposure_levels &lt;- levels(factor(data[[exposure]]))
  shifts &lt;- seq(-offset, offset, length.out = length(exposure_levels))
  data %&gt;%
    mutate(time_plot = .data[[time]] +
      shifts[match(as.character(.data[[exposure]]), exposure_levels)])
}


jitter_trajectories() changes only how the individual trajectories are drawn: it can move each participant’s whole line sideways, so overlapping lines separate, and it can move each plotted value up or down, so exact values cannot be read off. A fixed seed makes the result reproducible. Model means, confidence intervals, counts and p-values always come from the unchanged data.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Display-only changes to the individual trajectories (issue #11). Model
# means, confidence intervals, counts and p values always come from the
# unchanged data.
# - jitter_x: each participant's whole trajectory moves left or right by one
#   random amount, up to this many months, so overlapping lines separate
#   without zigzagging between visits.
# - jitter_y: each plotted value moves up or down by a random amount, up to
#   this much in the outcome's units, so exact values cannot be read off.
#   Whether to use it, and how much, is a disclosure decision (#11).
# A fixed seed makes a jittered figure reproducible, without changing R's
# random numbers anywhere else.
jitter_trajectories &lt;- function(trajectories, outcome, jitter_x = 0,
                                jitter_y = 0, seed = 20261108) {
  if (jitter_x == 0 && jitter_y == 0) {
    return(trajectories)
  }
  withr::with_seed(seed, {
    ids &lt;- unique(trajectories$study_id_screening)
    shifts &lt;- stats::setNames(
      stats::runif(length(ids), -jitter_x, jitter_x), ids
    )
    trajectories %&gt;%
      mutate(
        time_plot = time_plot + shifts[as.character(study_id_screening)],
        "{outcome}" := .data[[outcome]] +
          stats::runif(n(), -jitter_y, jitter_y)
      )
  })
}

# The visits on the x-axis, with room either side for the offsets. The
# panels zoom with coord_cartesian() rather than scale limits, so text placed
# beyond the last visit (the arm names) is kept, not dropped.
trajectory_x_scale &lt;- function(visits) {
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = visits, expand = expansion(0))
}

trajectory_coord &lt;- function(visits, y_limits = NULL) {
  coord_cartesian(
    xlim = range(visits) + c(-0.6, 0.6), ylim = y_limits, clip = "off"
  )
}

# A p value for display (issue #33): "&lt;0.001" below 0.001, otherwise three
# decimals ("0.040", "1.000"), and blank when there is none. Only the label
# is rounded; the estimates keep full precision.
format_p_value &lt;- function(p) {
  ifelse(
    is.na(p), "",
    ifelse(p &lt; 0.001, "&lt;0.001", sprintf("%.3f", p))
  )
}

# The p values of a set of contrasts as labels, with † for the follow-up
# contrasts (change from baseline); the row's heading says they are p values
format_p_labels &lt;- function(effects, dagger = TRUE) {
  effects %&gt;%
    mutate(time_linear = as.numeric(as.character(time_factor))) %&gt;%
    mutate(p_value_label = format_p_value(p.value)) %&gt;%
    mutate(p_value_label = ifelse(
      time_linear == 0 | !dagger | p_value_label == "",
      p_value_label,
      paste0(p_value_label, "\u2020")
    ))
}

# Rows for one level of the modifier, or every row when there is none
rows_for_level &lt;- function(rows, modifier, level) {
  if (is.null(modifier)) {
    rows
  } else {
    rows %&gt;% filter(as.character(.data[[modifier]]) == level)
  }
}




Means
plot_means() draws each arm’s model-estimated mean and 95% confidence interval at each visit, optionally over the individual trajectories. The arms differ by colour, point shape and line type, and are named at the end of their lines rather than in a legend.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Model-estimated mean (95% CI) for each arm at each visit, optionally over
# the individual trajectories. Arms differ by
# colour, shape and line type; label_arms names them at the right-hand end of
# their lines instead of in a legend. Returns a ggplot.
plot_means &lt;- function(data, estimates, outcome, modifier = NULL,
                       level = NULL, y_limits, show_trajectories = TRUE,
                       label_arms = TRUE, show_y_title = TRUE,
                       show_x_axis = FALSE, title = NULL, offset = 0.18,
                       jitter_x = 0, jitter_y = 0, seed = 20261108) {
  visits &lt;- sort(unique(data$time_linear))
  means &lt;- rows_for_level(estimates$means, modifier, level) %&gt;%
    mutate(time_linear = as.numeric(as.character(time_factor))) %&gt;%
    add_plot_offset(offset)

  panel &lt;- ggplot(means, aes(
    x = time_plot, y = estimate, colour = studyarm, group = studyarm
  ))

  if (show_trajectories) {
    trajectories &lt;- rows_for_level(data, modifier, level) %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(.data[[outcome]])) %&gt;%
      add_plot_offset(offset) %&gt;%
      jitter_trajectories(outcome, jitter_x, jitter_y, seed)
    panel &lt;- panel +
      geom_line(
        data = trajectories,
        mapping = aes(y = .data[[outcome]], group = study_id_screening),
        alpha = 0.2, linewidth = 0.3
      ) +
      geom_point(
        data = trajectories,
        mapping = aes(y = .data[[outcome]], group = study_id_screening),
        alpha = 0.2, size = 0.8
      )
  }

  panel &lt;- panel +
    geom_line(aes(linetype = studyarm), linewidth = 0.7) +
    geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = conf.low, ymax = conf.high),
                  width = 0, linewidth = 0.6) +
    geom_point(aes(shape = studyarm), fill = "white", size = 2, stroke = 0.7)

  if (label_arms) {
    last_visit &lt;- means %&gt;% filter(time_linear == max(time_linear))
    panel &lt;- panel +
      geom_text(
        data = last_visit,
        mapping = aes(
          x = max(visits) + 0.75, label = arm_labels[as.character(studyarm)]
        ),
        hjust = 0, size = 2.9, fontface = "bold"
      )
  }

  panel +
    trajectory_x_scale(visits) +
    trajectory_coord(visits, y_limits) +
    labs(
      x = if (show_x_axis) figure_label("time_linear") else NULL,
      y = if (show_y_title) figure_label(outcome) else NULL,
      title = title
    ) +
    theme_publication() +
    theme(
      legend.position = "none",
      axis.text.x = if (show_x_axis) {
        element_text(colour = "grey30")
      } else {
        element_blank()
      },
      axis.text.y = if (show_y_title) {
        element_text(colour = "grey30")
      } else {
        element_blank()
      }
    )
}




Effects
plot_effects() draws the effect at each follow-up visit with its 95% confidence interval and a line at zero: either the intervention minus control difference in change from baseline, or how much that effect differs between a subgroup level and the first level.


Click to show/hide R Code
# The effect at each follow-up visit with its 95% CI and a line at zero:
# intervention minus control in change from baseline ("within"), or how much
# that effect differs between a modifier level and the first level
# ("between"). Returns a ggplot.
plot_effects &lt;- function(estimates, visits, modifier = NULL, level = NULL,
                         comparison = NULL, y_limits, show_y_title = TRUE,
                         title = NULL) {
  if (is.null(comparison)) {
    effects &lt;- rows_for_level(estimates$within_levels, modifier, level)
    colour &lt;- publication_colours[2]
  } else {
    effects &lt;- estimates$between_levels %&gt;%
      filter(as.character(comparison) == .env$comparison)
    colour &lt;- publication_colours[3]
  }
  effects &lt;- effects %&gt;%
    mutate(time_linear = as.numeric(as.character(time_factor))) %&gt;%
    filter(time_linear &gt; 0)

  panel &lt;- ggplot(effects, aes(x = time_linear, y = estimate)) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = "grey40", linewidth = 0.4) +
    geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = conf.low, ymax = conf.high),
                  width = 0, linewidth = 0.6, colour = colour) +
    geom_point(size = 2, colour = colour)

  panel +
    trajectory_x_scale(visits) +
    trajectory_coord(visits, y_limits) +
    labs(
      x = figure_label("time_linear"),
      y = if (show_y_title) {
        "Difference (95% CI)"
      } else {
        NULL
      },
      title = title
    ) +
    theme_publication() +
    theme(
      axis.text.y = if (show_y_title) {
        element_text(colour = "grey30")
      } else {
        element_blank()
      }
    )
}




P-values and numbers observed
plot_p_values() draws a row of p-values aligned to the visits, under a heading saying which comparison they test; with three or more levels, alternate values sit on a second line so they do not overlap. format_p_value() writes each one to three decimals, or as “&lt;0.001”. plot_numbers_table() draws the number of participants with the outcome recorded, one row per arm, in the same columns as the visits above.


Click to show/hide R Code
# A row of p values aligned to the visits, under a heading: the effect
# within a level (marked † at follow-up, where it concerns change from
# baseline) or the difference between levels. With stagger = TRUE, every
# second visit's label sits on a second line, so labels on narrow panels do
# not run into each other. Returns a ggplot.
plot_p_values &lt;- function(effects, visits, title = NULL, dagger = TRUE,
                          show_baseline_p = TRUE, stagger = FALSE) {
  p_values &lt;- format_p_labels(effects, dagger = dagger) %&gt;%
    mutate(line = if (stagger) {
      -((match(time_linear, visits) - 1) %% 2)
    } else {
      0
    })
  if (!show_baseline_p) {
    p_values &lt;- p_values %&gt;% filter(time_linear != 0)
  }
  ggplot(p_values, aes(x = time_linear, y = line, label = p_value_label)) +
    geom_text(size = 2.3, colour = "grey20") +
    trajectory_x_scale(visits) +
    trajectory_coord(
      visits, y_limits = if (stagger) c(-1.4, 0.4) else c(-0.4, 0.4)
    ) +
    labs(title = title) +
    theme_void() +
    theme(plot.title = element_text(size = 7.5, colour = "grey20"))
}




Click to show/hide R Code
# Number of participants with the outcome recorded, per arm and visit: one
# row per arm, named in text, aligned to the visits above. Returns a ggplot.
plot_numbers_table &lt;- function(data, outcome, modifier = NULL, level = NULL,
                               show_row_labels = TRUE, title = "Observed, n") {
  visits &lt;- sort(unique(data$time_linear))
  counts &lt;- rows_for_level(data, modifier, level) %&gt;%
    filter(!is.na(.data[[outcome]])) %&gt;%
    count(time_linear, studyarm, name = "n_obs") %&gt;%
    mutate(arm = factor(
      arm_labels[as.character(studyarm)], levels = rev(arm_labels)
    ))

  ggplot(counts, aes(
    x = time_linear, y = arm, label = n_obs, colour = studyarm
  )) +
    geom_text(size = 2.7) +
    trajectory_x_scale(visits) +
    trajectory_coord(visits) +
    labs(x = NULL, y = NULL, title = title) +
    theme_publication() +
    theme(
      legend.position = "none",
      panel.grid.major.y = element_blank(),
      axis.line.x = element_blank(),
      axis.ticks.x = element_blank(),
      axis.text.x = element_blank(),
      axis.text.y = if (show_row_labels) {
        element_text(colour = "grey20", face = "bold")
      } else {
        element_blank()
      },
      plot.title = element_text(face = "plain", size = rel(0.9))
    )
}




The whole figure
compose_trajectory_figure() arranges the parts in one grid, so the visits line up from top to bottom: one column per subgroup level (a single column without a subgroup), with rows for the means, the effects, the p-values and the numbers table. Four options choose what a figure shows (#35):








Option
Default
What it does




show_trajectories
TRUE
Draws individual participants behind the means. Plots of real individual records are not published


show_p
TRUE
Adds rows of p-values for the effect within each level and the difference between levels


show_baseline_p
TRUE
Includes the baseline p-value. CONSORT advises against testing baseline differences in randomised trials


show_effect_panel
FALSE
Adds the effects row, with a column for each difference between levels



Three more settings change only what is displayed, for readability or disclosure (#11). Their defaults change nothing; the values for public figures are still to be decided with the disclosure rules. With vertical jitter, the y-axis widens by the same amount, so every displaced value stays on it. A data set with cells small enough to need suppressing should not be shown in this figure at all.








Setting
Default
What it does




jitter_x
0
Moves each participant’s trajectory sideways by up to this many months


jitter_y
0
Moves each plotted value up or down by up to this much, in the outcome’s units


seed
20261108
Makes the jitter reproducible



The result is a patchwork object. To change every panel, add to it with &, for example figure & theme(text = element_text(family = "Arial")); to change one panel, index it, for example figure[[1]]. For general guidance on writing and reading reusable recipes, see the Functions chapter in R for Data Science (Wickham et al. 2023).


Click to show/hide R Code
# The whole figure: one column per modifier level (a single column without a
# modifier), rows for the means, the effects and the numbers table, built as
# one grid so the visits line up from top to bottom. The four options are
# chosen per figure (#35); the defaults reproduce the earlier figure's
# content. jitter_x and jitter_y change only what is displayed (#11); both
# default to no change. A data set with cells small enough to need
# suppressing should not be shown in this figure at all. Returns a patchwork object: change every
# panel with `&`, or one panel by indexing it.
compose_trajectory_figure &lt;- function(data, estimates, outcome,
                                      modifier = NULL, title = NULL,
                                      show_trajectories = TRUE,
                                      show_p = TRUE,
                                      show_baseline_p = TRUE,
                                      show_effect_panel = FALSE,
                                      offset = 0.18, jitter_x = 0,
                                      jitter_y = 0, seed = 20261108) {
  visits &lt;- sort(unique(data$time_linear))
  if (is.null(modifier)) {
    levels_shown &lt;- list(NULL)
    comparisons &lt;- character()
  } else {
    data &lt;- data %&gt;% filter(!is.na(.data[[modifier]]))
    levels_shown &lt;- as.list(levels(data[[modifier]]))
    comparisons &lt;- unique(as.character(estimates$between_levels$comparison))
  }
  n_levels &lt;- length(levels_shown)

  # One y range for every means panel, widened by any vertical jitter so
  # every displaced value stays on the axis
  means_limits &lt;- range(
    estimates$means$conf.low, estimates$means$conf.high,
    if (show_trajectories) data[[outcome]] + jitter_y,
    if (show_trajectories) data[[outcome]] - jitter_y,
    na.rm = TRUE
  )

  level_title &lt;- function(level) {
    if (is.null(level)) NULL else paste0(figure_label(modifier), ": ", level)
  }
  spacers &lt;- function(n) rep(list(plot_spacer()), n)

  # Extra columns: one per between-level comparison with the effect panel,
  # otherwise one narrow column that leaves room for the arm labels
  extra &lt;- if (show_effect_panel && length(comparisons) &gt; 0) {
    length(comparisons)
  } else {
    1
  }
  widths &lt;- c(
    rep(1, n_levels),
    rep(if (show_effect_panel && length(comparisons) &gt; 0) 1 else 0.3, extra)
  )

  means_row &lt;- c(
    lapply(seq_len(n_levels), function(i) {
      plot_means(
        data, estimates, outcome, modifier, levels_shown[[i]],
        y_limits = means_limits, show_trajectories = show_trajectories,
        label_arms = i == n_levels, show_y_title = i == 1,
        show_x_axis = !show_effect_panel,
        title = level_title(levels_shown[[i]]), offset = offset,
        jitter_x = jitter_x, jitter_y = jitter_y, seed = seed
      )
    }),
    spacers(extra)
  )
  rows &lt;- list(means_row)
  heights &lt;- 3

  if (show_effect_panel) {
    effect_rows &lt;- bind_rows(
      estimates$within_levels, estimates$between_levels
    ) %&gt;%
      filter(as.numeric(as.character(time_factor)) &gt; 0)
    effect_limits &lt;- range(effect_rows$conf.low, effect_rows$conf.high, 0)
    effects_row &lt;- lapply(seq_len(n_levels), function(i) {
      plot_effects(
        estimates, visits, modifier, levels_shown[[i]],
        y_limits = effect_limits, show_y_title = i == 1,
        title = if (i == 1) {
          "Intervention minus control, change from baseline"
        } else {
          NULL
        }
      )
    })
    if (length(comparisons) &gt; 0) {
      effects_row &lt;- c(effects_row, lapply(comparisons, function(comparison) {
        plot_effects(
          estimates, visits, comparison = comparison,
          y_limits = effect_limits, show_y_title = FALSE,
          title = paste("Difference,", comparison)
        )
      }))
    } else {
      effects_row &lt;- c(effects_row, spacers(extra))
    }
    rows &lt;- c(rows, list(effects_row))
    heights &lt;- c(heights, 1.8)
  }

  if (show_p) {
    # Three or more levels leave too little width per visit for the labels
    # on one line (#33)
    stagger &lt;- n_levels &gt;= 3
    p_height &lt;- if (stagger) 0.6 else 0.4
    within_title &lt;- "p, intervention vs control (\u2020 change from baseline)"
    within_row &lt;- lapply(seq_len(n_levels), function(i) {
      plot_p_values(
        rows_for_level(estimates$within_levels, modifier, levels_shown[[i]]),
        visits, title = if (i == 1) within_title else NULL,
        show_baseline_p = show_baseline_p, stagger = stagger
      )
    })
    between_rows &lt;- lapply(comparisons, function(comparison) {
      plot_p_values(
        estimates$between_levels %&gt;%
          filter(as.character(.data$comparison) == .env$comparison),
        visits,
        title = if (show_effect_panel) paste("p,", comparison) else NULL,
        dagger = FALSE, show_baseline_p = show_baseline_p, stagger = stagger
      )
    })
    if (show_effect_panel && length(comparisons) &gt; 0) {
      # Between-level p values sit under their own effect columns
      rows &lt;- c(rows, list(c(within_row, between_rows)))
      heights &lt;- c(heights, p_height)
    } else {
      rows &lt;- c(rows, list(c(within_row, spacers(extra))))
      heights &lt;- c(heights, p_height)
      if (length(comparisons) &gt; 0) {
        stopifnot(length(comparisons) == n_levels - 1)
        # Each level's difference from the first sits under that level; the
        # heading takes the first level's column, which has no difference
        between_heading &lt;- ggplot() +
          labs(title = paste0(
            "p, difference between levels (each minus ",
            levels_shown[[1]], ")"
          )) +
          theme_void() +
          theme(plot.title = element_text(size = 7.5, colour = "grey20"))
        rows &lt;- c(
          rows, list(c(list(between_heading), between_rows, spacers(extra)))
        )
        heights &lt;- c(heights, p_height)
      }
    }
  }

  numbers_row &lt;- c(
    lapply(seq_len(n_levels), function(i) {
      plot_numbers_table(
        data, outcome, modifier, levels_shown[[i]],
        show_row_labels = i == 1, title = if (i == 1) "Observed, n" else NULL
      )
    }),
    spacers(extra)
  )
  rows &lt;- c(rows, list(numbers_row))
  heights &lt;- c(heights, 0.5)

  # What each element shows (issue #34)
  caption &lt;- c(
    if (show_trajectories) "Faint lines: each participant's recorded values.",
    if (show_trajectories && jitter_x &gt; 0) {
      "Trajectories are shifted sideways for readability."
    },
    if (show_trajectories && jitter_y &gt; 0) {
      "Individual values are randomly displaced; means use the exact values."
    },
    paste(
      "Points and bars: each arm's model-estimated mean and its 95%",
      "confidence interval (the uncertainty in the mean, not the spread of",
      "individual values)."
    ),
    if (show_effect_panel) {
      "Effects: intervention minus control in change from baseline (95% CI)."
    },
    if (show_p) {
      paste(
        "p values: at baseline, the difference between arms; at follow-up",
        "(\u2020), the difference between arms in change from baseline."
      )
    },
    if (show_p && length(comparisons) &gt; 0) {
      paste(
        "Between levels: each level's effect minus the first level's",
        "(the reference), not a test across all levels."
      )
    },
    if (show_p) "Not adjusted for multiple comparisons."
  )

  wrap_plots(unlist(rows, recursive = FALSE),
             ncol = length(widths), byrow = TRUE,
             widths = widths, heights = heights) +
    plot_annotation(
      title = title,
      caption = paste(strwrap(paste(caption, collapse = " "), 110),
                      collapse = "\n"),
      theme = theme_publication()
    )
}

Check the ggplot2 version
The theme relies on features added in ggplot2 4.0.0: a theme can carry the palettes its figures use, and labels can be looked up by variable name. On an older version the script stops with a message saying what to update, rather than failing later with a less helpful error.


